  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1029 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThe start of the long term forecast will begin with a more quiet and \nwarmer weekend compared to the past few days. Over the weekend, the \nupper ridging parked to the south of the forecast area will \ngradually expand north across our region as an upper trough \namplifies down the west coast. Subsidence below the ridging will \nkeep the region mostly dry Saturday and Sunday. There is a slight \nchance for showers and thunderstorms across areas of the Rolling \nPlains Saturday evening, however confidence on this occurring is \nlow. Height and thickness increases will warm temperatures back into \nthe 80s to upper 90s across the region. An upper pattern change next \nweek will bring a slight cool down and daily precipitation chances \nagain. The aforementioned upper trough will swing onshore off the \ncoast of Baja California and transition to a closed low across the \nDesert Southwest. This progression will once again shift \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, pumping in subtropical moisture. By mid-\nweek, models are in disagreement with the progression of the upper \nlow. ECMWF has a slower progression with the upper low stalling \nacross the Desert Southwest before reaching the region by the end of \nthe week. GFS has a faster track with the upper low crossing the \nTexas Panhandle by mid-week before deamplifying and moving away from \nthe region by the end of the week. Precipitation chances later in \nthe week will be dependent on the progression of the upper low.",
